<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <!-- Adult human thermal target, ~1.79 m, upright, origin between the feet, facing +x.

       WHY THE SEGMENTS HAVE DIFFERENT TEMPERATURES
       A real LWIR search-and-rescue signature is not a uniform human-shaped blob. Skin
       runs ~33-34 C, but clothing insulates: its outer surface settles far closer to
       ambient, and extremities colder still. So a person reads as a bright head and
       hands with a dim body - which is exactly what makes the detection problem
       non-trivial, and exactly what a single-threshold detector gets wrong. A uniform
       310 K mannequin would make every detector look good.

       WHY THESE PARTICULAR NUMBERS
       model://thermal_cam quantises heat sources to 2.57 K steps (ceiling/255 - see that
       model's notes), so segment temperatures cannot be chosen freely. Two rules came
       out of actually measuring it:

         1. Command values that sit ON a grid centre (count * 2.57), not near a
            boundary. Then the rendered value is exact and does not depend on how the
            GPU rounds.
         2. Space neighbours at least TWO counts apart. One count is not enough margin.

       Rule 2 was learned the hard way: the first cut used 306.15 K hands and 307.15 K
       head, only 1.0 K apart on a 2.57 K grid. Arithmetic said they would land on
       counts 119 and 120; in the render BOTH came back as 305.83 K and the head
       vanished as a distinct feature. A sub-step separation is a rounding accident, not
       a design.

         segment                 cmd K   count   reads K   vs 283.15 K ambient
         boots                  287.84     112    287.84      +4.7
         trousers / legs        292.98     114    292.98     +10.0
         clothed torso, arms    298.12     116    298.12     +15.0
         exposed hands          303.26     118    303.26     +20.1
         head / face            308.40     120    308.40     +25.3

       Commanded == rendered for all five, which is the point of rule 1. In C: boots
       14.7, legs 19.8, torso 25.0, hands 30.1, face 35.2 - all plausible for a clothed
       adult in 10 C air. If you retune these, move in whole steps of 2.57 K.

       <static>true</static>: this is scenery, and it sidesteps the failure mode
       thermal_cam has - a non-static model with mass and no collision falls through
       the ground plane and the sensor goes uniform. -->
  <model name='thermal_person'>
    <static>true</static>
    <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>

    <link name="body">
      <!-- One collision box for the whole torso+legs volume. Enough to stop a vehicle
           flying through the target; the limbs are visual-only. -->
      <collision name="collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.85 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box><size>0.30 0.50 1.70</size></box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- ===== BOOTS: 287.84 K, poorly perfused and in contact with cold ground ===== -->
      <visual name="boot_left">
        <pose>0.03 0.10 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.26 0.11 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.04 0.03 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.08 0.06 0.05 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>287.84</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
      <visual name="boot_right">
        <pose>0.03 -0.10 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.26 0.11 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.04 0.03 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.08 0.06 0.05 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>287.84</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>

      <!-- ===== LEGS: 292.98 K, trouser surface ===== -->
      <visual name="leg_left">
        <pose>0 0.10 0.50 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.075</radius><length>0.80</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.12 0.14 0.20 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.18 0.21 0.30 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>292.98</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
      <visual name="leg_right">
        <pose>0 -0.10 0.50 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.075</radius><length>0.80</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.12 0.14 0.20 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.18 0.21 0.30 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>292.98</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>

      <!-- ===== TORSO + UPPER ARMS: 298.12 K, jacket surface ===== -->
      <visual name="torso">
        <pose>0 0 1.20 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.26 0.42 0.62</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.20 0.09 0.05 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.35 0.15 0.08 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>298.12</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
      <visual name="upper_arm_left">
        <pose>0 0.255 1.30 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.052</radius><length>0.32</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.20 0.09 0.05 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.35 0.15 0.08 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>298.12</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
      <visual name="upper_arm_right">
        <pose>0 -0.255 1.30 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.052</radius><length>0.32</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.20 0.09 0.05 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.35 0.15 0.08 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>298.12</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>

      <!-- ===== FOREARMS + HANDS: 303.26 K, bare skin ===== -->
      <visual name="forearm_left">
        <pose>0 0.255 1.00 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.045</radius><length>0.30</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.45 0.32 0.24 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.72 0.53 0.40 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>303.26</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
      <visual name="forearm_right">
        <pose>0 -0.255 1.00 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.045</radius><length>0.30</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.45 0.32 0.24 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.72 0.53 0.40 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>303.26</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>

      <!-- ===== NECK + HEAD: 308.40 K, the hottest segment. From nadir this is the
                 top of the head, and it is often the ONLY segment that clears the
                 torso silhouette - see the nadir note at the end of this file. ===== -->
      <visual name="neck">
        <pose>0 0 1.55 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.048</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.45 0.32 0.24 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.72 0.53 0.40 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>308.40</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
      <visual name="head">
        <pose>0 0 1.685 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><sphere><radius>0.105</radius></sphere></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.45 0.32 0.24 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.72 0.53 0.40 1</diffuse>
        </material>
        <plugin filename="gz-sim-thermal-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Thermal">
          <temperature>308.40</temperature>
        </plugin>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- WHAT A NADIR CAMERA ACTUALLY SEES, measured at 20 m AGL with thermal_cam
         (18.65 m across 320 px, so ~58 mm/px and a whole standing adult is ~30 px):

           torso / shoulders   ~30 px    the bulk of the signature
           head                ~12 px    clears the torso outline, hottest
           hands                few px   forearms sit at y=+/-0.255 and the torso is
                                         only 0.21 half-wide, so barely a sliver shows
           legs, boots         0-1 px    directly underneath the torso, occluded

         So from straight above, this is a torso blob with a hot dot on it, and the leg
         and boot temperatures are effectively unused. They matter for an oblique or
         ground-level view. Do not tune the leg/boot values against a nadir image -
         you will be tuning something you cannot see. -->
  </model>
</sdf>
